==============================================================================
Gray rasterizer band-splitting regression harness (support/)
==============================================================================

This support tree adds a numeric regression for the STANDALONE_ smooth
(gray) rasterizer in src/smooth/ftgrays.c, focused on the horizontal
band-splitting / render-pool overflow replay path.  No font is loaded
and no GUI is built.  The production C source is compiled twice by
support/CMakeLists.txt, unchanged:

  ft_grays_raster        16 KiB cell pool (the default)
  ft_grays_raster_small   2 KiB cell pool (forces more, narrower bands
                          and more overflow bisections)

The C++17 test entry is support/target.cpp; it defines no main() (the
runner in support/runner.cpp drives the CASE-registered groups).  Run
everything with:

    bash support/run.sh target

It first runs the old plain-rectangle baseline, then the three groups
(bands, curves, layouts), then two isolated mutant builds of ftgrays.c
(drop_band: per-band min_ey += 1; lose_parity: ignore the even-odd
flag).  A mutant only counts as caught if the binary exits on a real
CHECK assertion; compile failure or a crash does not count.

Independent reference and its error bounds
-------------------------------------------

The rasterizer output is never trusted just because the two pool builds
agree (they would share any fill-rule bug).  target.cpp contains an
independent double-precision reference that calls none of FreeType's
decomposition, subdivision, winding or coverage code:

  * Beziers are flattened adaptively.  A segment is accepted only when
    every control point is within kFlat (1e-4 px) of its chord; each
    bisection shrinks that deviation by 4x, and subdivision is capped at
    24 levels.  The boundary displacement is therefore <= kFlat, and the
    area error of any pixel the edge crosses is below ~2*kFlat px^2,
    i.e. under 0.06 of a 0..255 gray level -- well inside the required
    0.5-gray reference budget.
  * For each pixel row, segment/scanline intersections are computed in
    double and combined with the requested topology: a running signed
    winding number for the non-zero rule, a parity toggle for
    even-odd.  Inside a y-slice without segment endpoints, each fill
    interval boundary is linear in y; the heights where a boundary
    crosses a column edge are solved directly and each piece is
    integrated exactly (trapezoid), so coverage is analytic for the
    flattened path.
  * The reference self-validates against a closed-form axis-aligned
    rectangle coverage formula (including a fractional rectangle and an
    exact 0/255 integer rectangle); its own measured error stays below
    0.5 gray.  A separate point-in-polygon supersampler was used during
    development to cross-check the analytic integration.

Comparison tolerances are deliberately tight and distinct:
straight-edged material must match within 1 gray level, fixed curve
cases within 4.  The tolerance is never widened to hide a missing row.

Why the band material is large enough
--------------------------------------

The bands fixture spans 140 px of height and carries 24 full-height
narrow strips plus disjoint solid blocks, producing ~35 contours and
well over a hundred points.  On the 2 KiB pool (512 cells) the initial
48-row bands overflow and are bisected repeatedly (48 -> 24 -> 12 -> 6
-> 3); trace builds show well over one hundred bisections, and the 16 KiB
pool also overflows and bisects its single band independently at
different cuts (about a dozen bisections).  Density is tuned so the
narrowest (3-row) band still fits, avoiding the rasterizer's "rotten
glyph" abort while guaranteeing multi-level replay.  Gentle curved
caps are placed on both sides of the initial cuts (48, 96) and the
bisection cuts, so a dropped first band row or a lost curve segment
changes pixels the independent reference catches.  Integer, negative
integer, and negative fractional 26.6 translations are all exercised.

Only short, wide, very shallow arcs are used for the curved material.
The production flattener's per-arc error grows with the arc bulge, so
cubic caps span >= 12 px with a 0.2 px bulge and conic caps use a
0.02 px bulge; these are genuine non-linear curves (the reference
integrates the exact Bezier), but stay inside the 4-gray budget on both
pools.  Straight strips and walls provide the band-overflow pressure.

What is not covered
-------------------

  * Steep or strongly curved (high-bulge) Beziers, S-shaped inflections
    with large excursions, and self-intersecting contours: the
    production flattener's approximation error on such geometry exceeds
    the 4-gray curve budget against an exact reference, so they are
    intentionally excluded rather than masked by a loose tolerance.
  * Even-odd area on overlapping sub-pixel boundaries (per the task
    scope); contours are kept disjoint with >= 2 px clearance.
  * LCD / LCD-V color modes, monochrome (1-bit) rendering, and any
    non-STANDALONE_ build path.
  * Dropout / stub control flags and hinting; the rasterizer ignores
    these in the exercised configuration.

